 depends on an unsupported turnover-cost assumption and an unestablished annual observation window. The candidate more responsibly explains the direction of compositional effects and explicitly warns that arithmetic gap reductions are not achievable intervention effects. This is better calibrated impact reasoning, although it stops short of estimating prospective benefits.",
