partmental interventions. The candidate more carefully prevents exclusion-check percentages from being interpreted as achievable reductions, while the baseline more explicitly discusses resource misallocation and KPI changes. Neither estimates intervention effects, costs, or likely retention gains with sufficient evidence to establish a clear comparative advantage on this sub-dimension.",
