{"gsb_ref_0": "{
  "Insight Presentation & Visualization": {
    "analysis": "Visual clarity & professionalism (+2): The candidate has a more cohesive, polished visual style: consistent teal bars, restrained gridlines, clear typographic hierarchy, and readable horizontal role labels. The baseline's large multipanel figures use more competing colors, a prominent red line, and lengthy diagonal labels; its pie chart also places black percentage labels over dark slices, reducing contrast. However, the candidate leaves substantial unused space and omits percentage units, axis titles, and direct value labels in both charts, whereas the baseline generally supplies these. Chart appropriateness & accuracy (+1): The candidate's zero-based department bars and descending horizontal role bars suit the comparison tasks, and their lengths agree with the accompanying tables. The baseline's standalone bars and composition pie are reasonable, but its dual-axis job-level/attrition overlay and connecting line across categorical roles introduce unnecessary interpretive complexity. The candidate clearly discloses its specialist grouping and manager exclusion. Its department chart nevertheless fails to visualize the uncertainty emphasized in its headline, relegating confidence intervals to the table. Textâvisual synergy & insightfulness (+1): Both reports place relevant narrative beside their figures. The candidate improves this connection with takeaway-oriented titles and surrounding explanations that distinguish department averages from role-level variation, highlighting the Sales Representative extreme and low-attrition specialist group. Its tables supply useful numerical context. Still, neither report explicitly guides readers through numbered figures or visual annotations, and much of the candidate's later narrative remains table-heavy rather than visually summarized. Overall, the candidate offers a moderate presentation improvement, not an across-the-board transformation.",
    "score": 4
  }
}", "gsb_ref_1": "{
  "Insight Presentation & Visualization": {
    "analysis": "Visual clarity & professionalism (+2): The candidate's two charts have a cohesive, polished style, with restrained teal coloring, clear headline hierarchy, light gridlines, and readable category labels. Its compact, ranked role chart avoids the baseline's overlapping Manager bars and colliding value labels, and is easier to scan than the baseline's very tall figure. However, both candidate charts omit explicit percentage-axis labels and direct value labels, whereas the baseline supplies both. The candidate's generous empty space also reduces information density. Chart appropriateness & accuracy (+1): Both reports appropriately use zero-based bars for comparisons, and their plotted lengths generally match their respective surrounding text. The candidate improves the role comparison by giving each displayed category its own unambiguous bar, sorting descending, and explicitly disclosing the specialist grouping and Manager exclusion. This avoids the baseline's obscured department comparisons for Managers. Its additional department chart is useful, but the headline's uncertainty distinction is not visually encoded with confidence intervals; readers must consult the adjacent table. Grouping specialist roles also sacrifices some of the baseline's role-level detail. Textâvisual synergy & insightfulness (0): Both reports connect their role charts to the central contrast between high-attrition Sales Representatives and low-attrition R&D occupations. The candidate adds takeaway-led chart titles, explanatory subtitles, and a department chart placed next to the corresponding rate and uncertainty table. The baseline, however, more explicitly introduces its figure and follows it with a dedicated 'Key Takeaways from the Chart' section, while its direct percentage labels support immediate verification. Much of the candidate's later discussion remains table- and text-led rather than visually supported. Overall, the candidate offers a moderate improvement in visual polish and comparison readability, not a wholesale improvement across all presentation criteria.",
    "score": 3
  }
}", "gsb_ref_2": "{
  "Insight Presentation & Visualization": {
    "analysis": "Visual clarity & professionalism (+1): The candidate has a more polished, consistent visual style: restrained teal coloring, strong headline hierarchy, subtle gridlines, and clean horizontal role labels. Its tables and section structure also make comparisons easier to scan than the baselineâs dense bullet lists. However, the charts use excessive whitespace, omit explicit percentage-axis labels and direct value labels, and therefore sacrifice some readability compared with the baselineâs clearly labeled axes and annotated bars. Chart appropriateness & accuracy (0): Both reports use appropriate, zero-based bar charts with plotted magnitudes consistent with their accompanying text. The candidateâs descending horizontal role chart is particularly suitable for comparing long role names and identifying extremes; the baselineâs grouped overtime chart effectively supports within-department comparisons. Neither offers a decisive overall advantage. The candidateâs uncertainty-focused headline would be better supported by plotted confidence intervals rather than referring readers to a separate table. Textâvisual synergy & insightfulness (+1): The candidateâs takeaway-led titles and placement next to the corresponding discussion connect the visuals directly to the department gap and occupational differences. Its role chart makes the high-attrition Sales Representative group and low-attrition R&D specialist group immediately apparent, visually supporting the reportâs central narrative. The baseline also explicitly explains both charts through observation and interpretation paragraphs, so the improvement is moderate rather than substantial. Overall, the candidate improves polish and narrative focus, partly offset by weaker standalone quantitative labeling.",
    "score": 2
  }
}", "gsb_ref_3": "{
  "Insight Presentation & Visualization": {
    "analysis": "Visual clarity & professionalism (+1): The candidate has a more polished, cohesive visual style: restrained teal coloring, subtle gridlines, clear typographic hierarchy, and horizontal role labels that avoid the baselineâs heavily rotated department labels. Its tables also organize comparisons well. However, both candidate charts omit explicit percentage-axis labels and direct value labels, whereas the baseline supplies units, values, and, in its department chart, sample sizes. The candidateâs generous whitespace and narrow department bars also reduce information density. Chart appropriateness & accuracy (0): Both reports use suitable comparison charts with proportionate bar lengths and zero-based bar axes. The candidateâs descending horizontal role chart makes ranking and long labels easy to read, with grouping and exclusions explained in its subtitle. Its department chart mentions uncertainty but leaves the confidence intervals entirely to the accompanying table rather than displaying them. The baselineâs grouped overtime bars and ordered satisfaction lines are reasonable encodings and provide more immediately readable numerical detail. Overall these strengths and limitations balance. Textâvisual synergy & insightfulness (+1): The candidateâs takeaway-led titles and adjacent narrative make its two visuals a coherent progression from department averages to occupational differences, explicitly drawing attention to high- and low-attrition roles. The baseline also places charts alongside relevant explanations, but its satisfaction narrative says R&D outperforms HR at every level even though the plotted HR rate is lower at level 2; this weakens textâvisual consistency. The candidate nevertheless leaves substantial later comparisons in dense tables and prose, and neither report uses figure-number references or targeted chart annotations extensively. Overall, the candidate is modestly better in presentation rather than dramatically superior.",
    "score": 2
  }
}", "gsb_ref_4": "{
  "Insight Presentation & Visualization": {
    "analysis": "Visual clarity & professionalism (+2): The candidate's two charts have a more polished, consistent visual style than the baseline, with strong headline hierarchy, readable typography, restrained teal coloring, subtle gridlines, and clean spacing. The horizontal role chart accommodates long labels particularly well. The baseline is legible and includes axis titles and a legend, but its typography is smaller and its styling more basic. The candidate is not flawless: both charts omit an explicit percentage axis label and direct value labels, and the oversized chart cards leave considerable unused space. Chart appropriateness & accuracy (+2): The candidate uses zero-based bars for comparable attrition proportions, orders roles by rate, and plots values consistent with the surrounding tables. Its department and role views directly support comparison of the outcome of interest. The baseline instead combines satisfaction ratings, work-life-balance ratings, and salary-hike percentages on one shared scale; these incompatible units make cross-metric bar heights uninformative and compress the rating differences. The candidate's department chart could better communicate its uncertainty-focused headline by displaying confidence intervals rather than referring readers to the table. Textâvisual synergy & insightfulness (+2): The candidate places each chart beside the relevant discussion and uses takeaway-oriented titles to emphasize the department gap and role-level heterogeneity. Its role-chart subtitle explains the specialist grouping and manager exclusion, while nearby text supplies the exact rates and population shares. The baseline also interprets each displayed metric, but its generic chart title and mixed-metric display do less to make the central attrition comparison immediately visible. The candidate still relies heavily on tables and prose, lacks explicit numbered figure references, and leaves later comparisons unvisualized. Overall, it provides a substantial but not complete improvement in presentation.",
    "score": 6
  }
}"}

