    "analysis": "Multi-angle analysis (+4/4): The candidate goes substantially beyond the baseline's aggregate Watch Count ranking and unadjusted category comparisons. It examines per-listing attention, sample support, medians, joint attention-and-Showings performance, sensitivity to support thresholds, adjusted within-Location comparisons, promotion, publication age, and model sensitivity. The switch from total to average Watch Count changes the question rather than directly correcting the baseline's total-volume ranking; the candidate appropriately states its interpretation, although retaining a total-volume comparison would make the reports easier to reconcile. Professional reasoning and evidence (+3/3): The candidate supplies sample sizes, explicit controls, clustered confidence intervals, reference categories, and an explanatory-power statistic. It distinguishes descriptive and conditional associations from causal effects and demonstrates how raw floor and orientation differences weaken after adjustment. This is substantially stronger than the baseline's unsupported explanations involving customization preferences, views, privacy, and larger families. The additive regression remains exploratory for a skewed count outcome, and its sensitivity checks are summarized rather than fully documented, but these limitations do not erase the improvement. Practical, grounded insights (+2/2): The candidate recommends targeted investigation and controlled testing instead of asserting that prioritizing large layouts, high floors, or south-facing properties will increase Showings. It also identifies undocumented metric windows and explains why a Watch-to-Showing conversion rate is not defensible. Its numerical results and SQL references cannot be independently verified from the supplied reports alone; nevertheless, the presentation is substantially more traceable and appropriately qualified than the baseline. Anticipation of impact (+1/1): The candidate quantifies the attention leader's below-average Showings and the unfinished segment's above-average performance on both measures, explaining why attention-only prioritization may not improve viewing demand. It also explains why weak independent floor and orientation signals should receive less strategic weight, whereas the baseline predicts benefits without comparable qualification.",
